# Target C-II: good-hull singleton face charge

**Date:** 2026-10-05  
**Base main:** \`b9bf98d807134d6400a9eb2762e1a8145862d66e\`  
**Status:** proof-only full-core Type-II strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.

## Result

Let \(G_D\) be the current-main family of complete stopped trains with at least
one good unit loaded-hull end, and let \(S_G\) count the singleton trains in
\(G_D\). Use

\[
x_B=b-q,\qquad m_B=\#\{\hbox{nonunit loaded-hull sides}\},
\]
\[
P_5=\#\{\hbox{bounded pentagonal contact faces}\},\qquad
X:=Q-f=\sum_{\deg F\ge6}(\deg F-5).
\]

Then in the full-core Type-II loaded-chord branch,

\[
\boxed{S_G\le x_B+m_B+2P_5+4X+3.}
\tag{GH.1}
\]

This is a finite face/outer-occurrence charge. It uses no asymptotic depletion
argument.

Combining (GH.1) with the exact current-main hull-depletion identity removes
the singleton population from the load-bearing frontier. Put

\[
E_F:=P_F-q,\qquad G_F:=b-P_F,\qquad
\sigma_R:=2T_R-4\sqrt{R_4},
\]
let \(S_0=S-S_G\) be the singleton trains left after deleting \(G_D\), let
\(d_D\le2\) be the two-good-end correction, and put
\(R_{\rm bulk}=R_4-R_G\). Then

\[
\boxed{
13E_F+11G_F+8P_5+14X+2\sigma_R+5d_D+12
\ge
2P_F-8\sqrt{R_4}+10\sqrt{R_{\rm bulk}}+S_0+5k.
}
\tag{GH.2}
\]

Current main gives \(P_F/D\to\pi\),
\(R_4/D^2\to\Delta_2=\pi/(2\sqrt3)\), and
\(R_G=o(D^2)\). With
\(s=\sqrt{\Delta_2}\), (GH.2) yields

\[
\boxed{
13E_F+11G_F+8P_5+14X+2\sigma_R
\ge [2(\pi+s)-o(1)]D+S_0+5k.
}
\tag{GH.3}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\max\{E_F,G_F,P_5,X,\sigma_R\}
\ge
\left({\pi+s\over24}-o(1)\right)D.
}
\tag{GH.4}
\]

The coefficient is about \(0.1706\). No computation or verifier output is a
proof premise.

## Dependencies and scope

The finite charge uses only:

1. canonical full-core loaded-chord localization: every loaded-loaded unit
   contact is a consecutive side of the actual loaded force hull;
2. the current-main exact hull-side occurrence audit:
   every unit loaded-hull side is a good distinguished outer interval and the
   total terminal-occurrence capacity of bad outer intervals is
   \[
   B_{\rm bad}=x_B+m_B;
   \]
3. canonical stopped-train geometry: every bounded quadrilateral contact face
   is a strict unit rhombus, and a singleton train edge belongs to no strict
   rhombus.

The composition also uses the independently audited current-main hull-depletion
theorem and the exact synthesis identity

\[
5m_B+4S+2\sigma_R+5d_D
=
2b-8\sqrt{R_4}+5M_D+5k+2X.
\tag{2.1}
\]

The argument is scoped to the full-core branch, where the strict-rhombus
population is nonzero for sufficiently large sources. It does not assert the
face sublemma for a degenerate all-shell contact cycle.

## 1. Bridge good-hull singletons

Fix a good-hull singleton train with physical edge \(e=uv\). Its endpoints
are consecutive loaded force-hull vertices, and one terminal incidence is the
good exterior hull occurrence.

If \(e\) is a bridge, its other terminal incidence is also on the outer face.
For \(q\ge3\), that second occurrence cannot also be good: the same unordered
physical edge cannot represent two distinct one-edge intervals between
consecutive loaded hull vertices. Therefore the second occurrence lies in a
bad outer interval.

Different singleton trains have distinct terminal occurrences. Thus, if
\(S_G^{\rm br}\) counts bridge good-hull singletons,

\[
\boxed{S_G^{\rm br}\le x_B+m_B.}
\tag{3.1}
\]

The \(m_B\) term is real. A hull path with one missing closing hull contact is
the finite regression: its good singleton bridges use the exact bad-interval
capacity \(x_B+m_B\), while \(x_B\) alone is smaller.

## 2. Nonbridge good-hull singletons

If \(e\) is not a bridge, its exterior incidence is the unbounded face and its
other incidence is one bounded face \(F\). That face is not quadrilateral,
because every bounded quadrilateral is a strict rhombus and then \(e\) would
not be singleton. Triangle-freeness excludes degree three. Hence
\(\deg F\ge5\).

Assign \(e\) to this unique bounded exceptional face. For such a face of
degree \(d\), let \(r(F)\) be the number of its boundary edges that are unit
loaded-hull sides.

In the full-core branch,

\[
\boxed{r(F)\le d-2.}
\tag{4.1}
\]

If \(r(F)=d-1\), deleting the one non-hull edge leaves a path of loaded-hull
sides, so the remaining edge joins two loaded vertices. It is a unit contact.
The loaded-chord theorem makes it a consecutive loaded-hull side as well,
contradicting \(r(F)=d-1\).

If \(r(F)=d\), the whole facial cycle is the loaded-hull cycle. As a face of
the contact plane graph it leaves no strict rhombus in its interior, the shell
degeneration excluded in the present full-core branch.

For \(d\ge6\),

\[
d-2\le4(d-5),
\]

so all degree-at-least-six exceptional faces absorb at most

\[
\boxed{\sum_{\deg F\ge6}r(F)\le4X.}
\tag{4.2}
\]

## 3. Pentagon sharpening

For a pentagon, (4.1) gives \(r(F)\le3\). If \(r(F)=3\), those three hull
sides must be consecutive around the face.

Indeed, suppose they form \(c\ge2\) maximal hull-side chains. Every gap
between two chains is a contact path with loaded endpoints. A one-edge gap
would be a loaded-loaded unit contact and, by loaded-chord localization,
another hull side, contradicting maximality. Each gap therefore has at least
two non-hull edges. But a pentagon with three hull edges has only two non-hull
edges total. Thus \(c=1\).

Let \(e_1,e_2,e_3\) be the three consecutive oriented unit hull-side vectors,
and let \(\Theta\) be the total exterior hull turn across their two internal
vertices. The other two unit edges of the pentagon join the same endpoints, so

\[
|e_1+e_2+e_3|\le2.
\tag{5.1}
\]

If \(\Theta\le\pi\), projection onto the bisector of the first and third side
directions gives

\[
|e_1+e_2+e_3|\ge3\cos(\Theta/2).
\]

If \(\Theta>\pi\), the next conclusion is automatic. Therefore

\[
\boxed{\Theta\ge2\arccos(2/3)>\pi/2.}
\tag{5.2}
\]

Distinct \(r(F)=3\) pentagons use edge-disjoint three-edge hull chains, hence
their two internal hull-turn terms are disjoint. The total convex-hull turn is
\(2\pi\). Thus there are at most three such pentagons, and

\[
\boxed{\sum_{\deg F=5}r(F)\le2P_5+3.}
\tag{5.3}
\]

Here \(2\arccos(2/3)>\pi/2\) follows exactly from
\(2/3<1/\sqrt2\).

Combining (3.1), (4.2), and (5.3) proves (GH.1).

## 4. Eliminate singleton trains from the depletion frontier

Let \(S_0\) be the singleton trains among the \(M_D\) trains remaining after
deleting \(G_D\). A singleton is an isolated vertex of the strict-rhombus
train-crossing graph, so deleting all \(S_0\) removes no
\(R_{\rm bulk}\) crossing edge. Mantel gives

\[
M_D-S_0\ge2\sqrt{R_{\rm bulk}}.
\tag{6.1}
\]

Also \(S=S_G+S_0\). Insert these in (2.1):

\[
5m_B+4S_G+2\sigma_R+5d_D
\ge
2b-8\sqrt{R_4}+10\sqrt{R_{\rm bulk}}+S_0+5k+2X.
\tag{6.2}
\]

By (GH.1), the left side is at most

\[
9m_B+4x_B+8P_5+16X+2\sigma_R+5d_D+12.
\]

Therefore

\[
9m_B+4x_B+8P_5+14X+2\sigma_R+5d_D+12
\ge
2b-8\sqrt{R_4}+10\sqrt{R_{\rm bulk}}+S_0+5k.
\tag{6.3}
\]

The exact hull-side ledger gives \(x_B=m_B+u_B\) with \(u_B\ge0\), hence
\(m_B\le x_B\). Also

\[
x_B=b-q=(b-P_F)+(P_F-q)=G_F+E_F.
\tag{6.4}
\]

Unit separation gives \(E_F\ge0\), while polygonal outer-route length gives
\(G_F\ge0\). Use \(m_B\le x_B\) and \(b=P_F+G_F\) in (6.3), then subtract
\(2G_F\). This is exactly (GH.2).

Dividing by \(D\) with the current-main full-core limits proves (GH.3).
All five left-side currencies are nonnegative, and their coefficients sum to

\[
13+11+8+14+2=48.
\]

This proves (GH.4), with the stronger form

\[
\max\{E_F,G_F,P_5,X,\sigma_R\}
\ge
\left({\pi+s\over24}-o(1)\right)D+{S_0+5k\over48}.
\tag{6.5}
\]

If \(E_F,G_F,X,\sigma_R=o(D)\), then (GH.3) also gives

\[
\boxed{
P_5\ge{\pi+s\over4}D+{S_0+5k\over8}-o(D).
}
\tag{6.6}
\]

Thus a low-metric-defect, low-high-degree, low-train-slack survivor cannot
hide its defect in good-hull singleton trains; it must contain a linear
population of actual pentagonal contact faces.

## 5. Regression checks and remaining obligation

Three stronger-looking statements are not used.

* \(S_G^{\rm br}\le x_B\) is false in the hull-path regression; the exact
  \(x_B+m_B\) capacity is needed.
* A pentagon need not have at most two loaded-hull sides. Three consecutive
  hull sides are possible locally; the proof only shows that this event costs
  more than \(\pi/2\) of global hull turning, so it occurs at most three times.
* The \(r(F)\le d-2\) argument is not exported to the no-rhombus shell
  degeneration. The current full-core branch has quadratic strict-rhombus
  population.

No source point is deleted, no contact isolate is identified with a contact-core
vertex, and no infinitesimal motion is treated as a finite deformation.

The full-core higher-surplus branch is now forced to pay linearly in at least
one of

\[
P_F-q,\qquad b-P_F,\qquad P_5,\qquad Q-f,\qquad\sigma_R.
\]

The remaining Target C step is to spend one of these physical currencies
against the actual Woodall diameter graph, construct a same-cardinality
strict-\(D\) source, or force an exact unit equilateral triangle. The
macroscopic-container branch is unchanged. C-II and Level I remain open.

